Thanks a bunch for the clarification Eric, I was going a bit crazy there for a little while trying to figure out what I was doing different in R, but if the likelihood estimation is slightly different between the two programs that makes a lot of sense. Knowing that I now probably will just go with my R results but I'll definitely check out readdst as well.

Thanks for the help!

On Wednesday, December 14, 2016 at 2:19:01 AM UTC-7, Eric Rexstad wrote:
>
> Nathaniel
>
> The software performing the maximum likelihood estimation of the detection function parameters differs between the GUI-software Distance and the R-based software; consequently for some complex (high number of parameter) models, the estimates will be slightly different.
> Regarding debug mode and the existence of input files.  The files you describe "in.r", "ddf.dat.r", etc. are only created when Distance is using analysis engines such as MRDS or DSM; i.e. engines that use R software.  Your earlier message described your use of MCDS, which is written in Fortran rather than R, as a consequence, no ".r" files are created when MCDS analyses are run in debug mode.
>
> If you are truly keen on making comparisons between MCDS results and results of similar models fit using R, I direct you to an experimental R package, "readdst" (available via github) that reads existing GUI-Distance projects, translates their data structures to R-type structures, and can perform estimation of analyses in the GUI-Distance project in R.  This package is a "work in progress" and requires some reasonable sophistication in the use of R.  There is a vignette describing its capabilities here
> http://distancesampling.org/R/vignettes/Project-migration.html
>
> On 13/12/2016 19:01, Nathaniel Behl wrote:
> > Laura,
> > Thanks, that's really helpful! I modified the distbegin/end columns and got the ddf to work. However, I have a quick follow-up question... I notice that the results (i.e. AIC value, parameter estimates, etc) are all slightly different from the the results I obtain in Distance. I'm assuming this is because the model I ran was slightly different, but I haven't been able to figure out how.
> >
> > Using debug mode as you suggested seems like the ideal way to solve this problem as I'd be able to actually look at the code Distance is using and see how it's different from mine. However, I can't find the files in.r, ddf.dat.r, etc that the manual says should be created when I run Distance in debug mode. I can find the input and data "dst" temp files that are created which are located exactly where log tab says they should be but can't access any of the other described files that are necessary to copy this analysis to R. Are these files somehow contained in the .temp files or are they being stored somewhere else not listed in the log tab?
> >
> > Thanks again,
> > Nathaniel
> >
> > On Tuesday, December 13, 2016 at 4:19:11 AM UTC-7, distance wrote:
